            {
              "text": "{\"problem\": {\"theorem\": \"Consider a prime $p$ of the form $4 t+3$. Show that $a$ is a primitive root modulo $p$ iff $-a$ has order $(p-1) / 2$.\", \"assumptions\": [], \"domain\": \"number_theory\", \"explicit_subgoals\": []}, \"candidates\": [{\"candidate_id\": \"choice-1\", \"text\": \"Let \\\\(p=4t+3\\\\) and set \\\\(m=(p-1)/2=2t+1\\\\), which is odd.\\n\\nSuppose first that \\\\(a\\\\) is a primitive root modulo \\\\(p\\\\). Then \\\\(\\\\operatorname{ord}_p(a)=p-1=2m\\\\). Since \\\\(a^{2m}\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod p\\\\) and \\\\(a^m\\\\not\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod p\\\\), while \\\\((a^m)^2\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod p\\\\), we have \\\\(a^m\\\\equiv-1\\\\pmod p\\\\). Hence\\n\\\\[\\n(-a)^m=(-1)^m a^m\\\\equiv(-1)(-1)\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod p.\\n\\\\]\\nThus \\\\(\\\\operatorname{ord}_p(-a)\\\\mid m\\\\). Conversely, if \\\\((-a)^n\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod p\\\\), then \\\\(a^{2n}=(-a)^{2n}\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod p\\\\). Since \\\\(\\\\operatorname{ord}_p(a)=2m\\\\), this implies \\\\(2m\\\\mid2n\\\\), hence \\\\(m\\\\mid n\\\\). Therefore \\\\(\\\\operatorname{ord}_p(-a)=m=(p-1)/2\\\\).\\n\\nConversely, suppose \\\\(\\\\operatorname{ord}_p(-a)=m\\\\). Then\\n\\\\[\\na^m=(-1)^m(-a)^m\\\\equiv-1\\\\pmod p,\\n\\\\]\\nbecause \\\\(m\\\\) is odd. Also \\\\(a^{2m}\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod p\\\\). If \\\\(a^n\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod p\\\\), then \\\\((-a)^{2n}=a^{2n}\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod p\\\\), so \\\\(m\\\\mid2n\\\\). Since \\\\(m\\\\) is odd, \\\\(m\\\\mid n\\\\); write \\\\(n=mq\\\\). Now\\n\\\\[\\n1\\\\equiv a^n=(a^m)^q\\\\equiv(-1)^q\\\\pmod p.\\n\\\\]\\nAs \\\\(p\\\\) is odd, this forces \\\\(q\\\\) to be even. Hence \\\\(2m\\\\mid n\\\\). Since \\\\(a^{2m}\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod p\\\\), it follows that \\\\(\\\\operatorname{ord}_p(a)=2m=p-1\\\\). Thus \\\\(a\\\\) is a primitive root modulo \\\\(p\\\\).\"}, {\"candidate_id\": \"choice-2\", \"text\": \"Let \\\\(m=(p-1)/2=2t+1\\\\), which is odd.\\n\\nSuppose first that \\\\(a\\\\) is a primitive root modulo \\\\(p\\\\), so \\\\(\\\\operatorname{ord}_p(a)=p-1=2m\\\\). Since \\\\((a^m)^2\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod p\\\\) and \\\\(a^m\\\\not\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod p\\\\), we have \\\\(a^m\\\\equiv-1\\\\pmod p\\\\). Hence\\n\\\\[\\n(-a)^m=(-1)^m a^m\\\\equiv(-1)(-1)\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod p.\\n\\\\]\\nIf \\\\((-a)^n\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod p\\\\), then \\\\(a^{2n}=(-a)^{2n}\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod p\\\\). Since \\\\(a\\\\) has order \\\\(2m\\\\), this implies \\\\(2m\\\\mid2n\\\\), hence \\\\(m\\\\mid n\\\\). Therefore the least positive such \\\\(n\\\\) is \\\\(m\\\\), so \\\\(\\\\operatorname{ord}_p(-a)=m=(p-1)/2\\\\).\\n\\nConversely, suppose \\\\(\\\\operatorname{ord}_p(-a)=m\\\\). Because \\\\(m\\\\) is odd,\\n\\\\[\\na^m=(-(-a))^m=-(-a)^m\\\\equiv-1\\\\pmod p,\\n\\\\]\\nso \\\\(a^{2m}\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod p\\\\). Now suppose \\\\(a^n\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod p\\\\). Then\\n\\\\[\\n(-a)^{2n}=a^{2n}\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod p,\\n\\\\]\\nso \\\\(m\\\\mid2n\\\\). Since \\\\(m\\\\) is odd, \\\\(m\\\\mid n\\\\); write \\\\(n=qm\\\\). Then\\n\\\\[\\n1\\\\equiv a^n=(a^m)^q\\\\equiv(-1)^q\\\\pmod p.\\n\\\\]\\nAs \\\\(p\\\\) is odd, this forces \\\\(q\\\\) to be even. Thus \\\\(2m\\\\\\\\mid n\\\\). Since \\\\(a^{2m}\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod p\\\\), it follows that \\\\(\\\\operatorname{ord}_p(a)=2m=p-1\\\\). Hence \\\\(a\\\\) is a primitive root modulo \\\\(p\\\\).\"}, {\"candidate_id\": \"choice-3\", \"text\": \"Let \\\\(m=(p-1)/2=2t+1\\\\), which is odd.\\n\\nSuppose first that \\\\(a\\\\) is a primitive root modulo \\\\(p\\\\). Then \\\\(\\\\operatorname{ord}_p(a)=p-1=2m\\\\). Thus \\\\(a^{2m}\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod p\\\\), while \\\\(a^m\\\\not\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod p\\\\). Since \\\\((a^m)^2\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod p\\\\) and \\\\(p\\\\) is odd, it follows that \\\\(a^m\\\\equiv-1\\\\pmod p\\\\). Therefore\\n\\\\[\\n(-a)^m=(-1)^m a^m\\\\equiv(-1)(-1)\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod p.\\n\\\\]\\nHence \\\\(\\\\operatorname{ord}_p(-a)\\\\mid m\\\\). Conversely, if \\\\((-a)^n\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod p\\\\), then \\\\(a^{2n}=(-a)^{2n}\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod p\\\\). Since \\\\(\\\\operatorname{ord}_p(a)=2m\\\\), we have \\\\(2m\\\\mid2n\\\\), so \\\\(m\\\\mid n\\\\). Consequently \\\\(\\\\operatorname{ord}_p(-a)=m=(p-1)/2\\\\).\\n\\nConversely, suppose \\\\(\\\\operatorname{ord}_p(-a)=m\\\\). Put \\\\(b=-a\\\\), so \\\\(\\\\operatorname{ord}_p(b)=m\\\\) and \\\\(a=-b\\\\). Since \\\\(m\\\\) is odd,\\n\\\\[\\na^{2m}=(-b)^{2m}=b^{2m}\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod p.\\n\\\\]\\nNow suppose \\\\(a^n\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod p\\\\). If \\\\(n\\\\) is even, then \\\\(b^n=(-1)^n a^n\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod p\\\\), so \\\\(m\\\\mid n\\\\). Because \\\\(m\\\\) is odd and \\\\(n\\\\) is even, this gives \\\\(2m\\\\mid n\\\\).\\n\\nIf \\\\(n\\\\) is odd, then \\\\(b^n=(-1)^n a^n\\\\equiv-1\\\\pmod p\\\\). Squaring gives \\\\(b^{2n}\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod p\\\\), so \\\\(m\\\\mid2n\\\\). Since \\\\(m\\\\) is odd, \\\\(m\\\\mid n\\\\), which would imply \\\\(b^n\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod p\\\\), a contradiction. Thus no odd \\\\(n\\\\) satisfies \\\\(a^n\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod p\\\\).\\n\\nTherefore every positive exponent \\\\(n\\\\) with \\\\(a^n\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod p\\\\) is divisible by \\\\(2m\\\\), while \\\\(a^{2m}\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod p\\\\). Hence \\\\(\\\\operatorname{ord}_p(a)=2m=p-1\\\\), so \\\\(a\\\\) is a primitive root modulo \\\\(p\\\\).\"}]}",
              "text_elements": [],
              "type": "text"
            }
